		{
			"id": "e0CS1GlmHzeld0Uqg",
			"description": "Boltgun, Godwyn Pattern",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The Godwyn-De'az pattern is the boltgun the fiction treats as the baseline: a self-contained, rocket-assisted .75-calibre round whose motor ignites a few metres clear of the muzzle, widely depicted (Horus Heresy and modern Black Library alike) as capable of blowing an unarmoured man apart and reliably punching through carapace plate, while a Space Marine can shrug off a few hits from another Marine's boltgun before going down. Special Issue Ammunition (Kraken, Hellfire, Metal Storm, Vengeance) is Deathwatch's own term and rulebook material; Inferno bolts are that line's Deathwatch-specific incendiary load; sanctified/blessed ammunition is a recurring detail in Adepta Sororitas and Chaplain background with no stated mechanical difference against armour.\n\nDesign: This is the project's fixed boltgun anchor: 6dx2(2) pi++ with a 2d cr ex follow-up (per the framework's damage table; user-set minimum). The core is 2d because an explosive follow-up that penetrates bursts inside the body for triple damage (B414): about 21 average injury, much like the old 6d without the rule. Against Astartes power armour (torso DR 100) the armour divisor (2) drops effective DR to 50; 6dx2 averages 42, so it penetrates about 14% of hits (exact dice distribution) and still has to beat the wearer's HP with the pi++ wounding bonus and the 2d cr ex follow-up (tripled) when it does, matching \"a Marine takes several bolts to kill another Marine.\" Against carapace (DR 70, effective DR 35) it penetrates 79% of hits, against Adepta Sororitas armour (DR 85, effective DR 42.5) 45%, and against an unarmoured man or flak (DR 30, effective DR 15) it is always lethal - all as the framework specifies. Against Mk X Gravis (DR 130, effective DR 65) it drops to 0.2%, and it essentially never beats a Terminator (DR 200, effective DR 100). Kraken keeps the same 6dx2 dice but swaps the armour divisor to (3) and drops the wounding modifier from pi++ to pi (less internal fragmentation, more punch) and shrinks the follow-up from 2d to 1d-1 cr ex; it penetrates Astartes Mk VII (effective DR 33) on 85.5% of hits (standard bolt 14.5%), Gravis (effective DR 43) on 45.4% and a Terminator (effective DR 67) on only 0.1%, trading wounding multiplier and blast for a much better chance against Marine plate; it is still useless against Terminator armour, like the standard bolt. Hellfire and Metal Storm are built from Deathwatch's stated design intent (anti-organic, anti-horde) translated into GURPS damage types (tox for the bio-payload, a fragmentation bracket for the flechette cloud) rather than lifted from any catalogue entry: Hellfire keeps the standard round's x2(2) mechanism but halves the dice (3dx2(2) pi, since most of the penetrator mass is sacrificed for payload) and its tox follow-up (3d tox, organic targets only; the follow-up only happens after the bolt has penetrated, so it carries no armour divisor); Metal Storm is 7d pi [3d pi], a no-armour-divisor flechette cloud, lethal to unarmoured hordes and irrelevant against real plate. Vengeance and sanctified rounds are modelled as unchanged ballistically from the standard round (6dx2(2) pi++ / 2d cr ex), since neither RPG nor Codex material describes a different penetrator, with their special property left as a notes-level rule the GM applies. Inferno bolts keep the standard penetrator (6dx2(2) pi++) and give an 8d burn ex incendiary follow-up, matching the standard 2d follow-up's scale. Handling: this entry is also the project's fixed handling anchor for every Astartes-pattern boltgun on this page. Loaded weight is set to 38 lb per the framework's own guidance (an Astartes Godwyn boltgun runs about 35-40 lb loaded; Deathwatch lists 18 kg ≈ 39.7 lb). Min ST 19 follows √(10×38)≈19.5, exactly the framework's worked example for \"a 36 lb Astartes boltgun\": a Guardsman (ST 10-11) can lift it but fires it at a heavy under-ST penalty two- handed, while a Marine at ST 30 comfortably exceeds 1.5×19=28.5 and fires it one-handed, as the fiction consistently shows. RoF 3 reflects the boltgun's actual cyclic action - a recoil-operated mechanism firing large, rocket-propelled cartridges, not a true high-cyclic autoloader - per framework.md's \"bolters fire slower, heavier rounds (boltgun 3 semi or burst)\" rule; every ammunition usage above shares this RoF and the same 24-round sickle magazine (Shots 24(3), the standard capacity across Black Library and Codex background) since swapping ammunition doesn't change the action. Bulk -5, one step worse than a human rifle's -4, per the framework's rule that Astartes-pattern weapons are a step bulkier than a human weapon of the same type; Rcl 3 matches framework.md's \"bolters 3-4\" band, and Acc 4 sits in the rifle range (4-5) appropriate to iron sights and a scope rail rather than a dedicated optic.",
			"tags": [
				"Missile Weapon",
				"Imperium",
				"Adeptus Astartes",
				"Astra Militarum"
			],
